      "commit": {
        "parents": [
          {
            "commit": "c0b1952267c3828a310d351e18c0238ea5718672",
            "subject": "LU-20306 scripts: lustre_rmmod wait to drain OBD devices"
          }
        ],
        "author": {
          "name": "Minh Diep",
          "email": "mdiep@thelustrecollective.com",
          "date": "2026-07-27 22:40:09.000000000",
          "tz": -420
        },
        "committer": {
          "name": "Minh Diep",
          "email": "mdiep@thelustrecollective.com",
          "date": "2026-07-28 01:06:55.000000000",
          "tz": -420
        },
        "subject": "LU-20306 scripts: retry rmmod until module refcnt drains",
        "message": "LU-20306 scripts: retry rmmod until module refcnt drains\n\nAn empty \"lctl dl\" does not imply module refcounts have reached\nzero: there is a short tail after the last OBD device disappears\nwhere mdt/osd/ptlrpc still hold a module reference, so a fast\nteardown can fail rmmod with \"module in use\" even after the device\ndrain added previously passes. OST-heavy hosts tend to escape this\nbecause their longer device drains outlast the tail; a fast\nsingle-MDT umount exposes it.\n\nShare the --wait budget between the device drain and per-module\nunload retries: on an rmmod failure, poll /sys/module/<mod>/refcnt\nonce per second and reattempt the unload only when it reads 0 (a\nmodule that vanished concurrently counts as unloaded). On budget\nexhaustion, make a final visible rmmod attempt so a genuinely stuck\nmodule still fails with the usual \"Module ... is in use\", preserving\nthe non-fatal-timeout semantics of the original wait.\n\n--wait 0 keeps the historical immediate single-attempt behavior.\n\nChange-Id: I28c5fd0108a09cb5ae5e4868fc77963ae034057a\nSigned-off-by: Minh Diep <mdiep@thelustrecollective.com>\n"
      },
      "branch": "refs/heads/master"
    }
  },
